    <section xml:id="sec-servo-day15">
        <title>Day 15 In-Class: Commanding a Servomotor</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we'll design the signal that commands a servomotor, and we'll
                produce it on the STM32C031C6.  We'll start with what is inside the
                servo and what its feedback loop does, then work out how TIM14 has to be
                set up for a 50 Hz pulse, and then build a program that turns the
                potentiometer's position into a pulse width and check that pulse on the
                AD2 oscilloscope.  At the end we'll look at how the servo is powered; we'll
                wire it up tomorrow.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 1: 6 min = 2 (recall) + 4 (push commit and reveal) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15-loop">
            <title>Part 1: A Motor with a Loop Inside It</title>
            <p>
                Inside the servo is a DC permanent-magnet motor, the same motor we
                studied in the motor chapter (<xref ref="subsec-day11-actuator-chain"/>),
                and one fact from that chapter is the key fact we need going forward:
                the torque a motor produces is proportional to the current through it. Now we'll add on to it.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-inside" ref="fig-servo-cutaway">
    <ul>
        <li>Inside the servo is a DC permanent-magnet motor, the same motor we studied in the motor chapter, and the key fact we need going forward is that the torque a motor produces is proportional to the current through it.</li>
        <li>The motor shaft drives a gear train, labeled GEARBOX in the cutaway, that reduces the speed by a large factor at the output shaft. Power is the product of torque and angular velocity and is (except for friction) preserved, so the input is high speed and low torque, and the output is low speed and high torque.</li>
        <li>A potentiometer on the output shaft gives the angular position of the shaft, and a control circuit with an H-bridge drives the motor.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 2 min, recall of the reading. The figure is the reading's cutaway (refPage). Do not re-teach the motor physics; name torque ∝ current and move on.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                The gear train trades the motor's speed for torque at the output shaft,
                and the potentiometer on that shaft is what closes the loop
                (<xref ref="fig-servo-feedback"/>): its voltage is compared with the command voltage, and the controller drives the motor until the difference is zero.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-loop" ref="fig-servo-feedback" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>The potentiometer on the output shaft is what closes the loop: its voltage is compared with the command voltage, and their difference is the error.</li>
        <li>The controller drives the motor until the error is zero. Our servomotors have the controller built in; what we supply is the command.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>The command goes in at the left; the potentiometer feeds the position back to the summing point.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min, then the activity. The reading's figure, by refPage.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-push">
                <title>What if something pushed on the horn?</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>Suppose the servo has reached its commanded position and is
                    holding it, and something pushes on the horn.  What does the servo
                    do, and what inside it makes that happen?  (Think it through; do not
                    try it on your servo.)</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <p>
                Once in its position, if something turns the horn, the potentiometer is
                going to sense that, and the controller is going to counteract that
                error.  So the servo will strongly resist.  That is also why the position
                can be measured with a potentiometer, which only turns part of a
                revolution, and why you never turn a servo by hand or take it all the way around: if you do, you break it.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-push-reveal" instructor="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>Once in its position, if something turns the horn, the potentiometer is going to sense that, and the controller is going to counteract that error. So the servo will strongly resist.</li>
        <li>That is also why the position can be measured with a potentiometer, which only turns part of a revolution.</li>
        <li>And it is why you never turn a servo by hand or take it all the way around: if you do, you break it.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>REVEAL, after the activity. ≈ 1 min of the Part's 4. Her slide 8 note, near verbatim.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2: 8 min = 5 (between-pulses commit, reveal, dead band) + 3 (Table 12) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15-command">
            <title>Part 2: How We Command the Servo</title>
            <p>
                The signal that commands the servo's position is the pulse from the reading (<xref ref="fig-servo-pulses"/>): 1 ms to 2 ms wide, repeated every 20 ms.  By convention 1.5 ms commands the middle position, and 1 ms and 2 ms command the two ends.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-pulse" ref="fig-servo-pulses" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>The signal that commands the servo's position is a pulse.</li>
        <li>It is 1 ms to 2 ms wide, repeated every 20 ms. By convention 1.5 ms commands the middle position, and 1 ms and 2 ms command the two ends.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min, then the between-pulses activity. Refs the reading's pulse figure (refPage).</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-between-pulses">
                <title>Between the pulses</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>The pulse is at most 2 ms wide and the period is 20 ms, so for
                    most of every period the signal is LOW.  What does the servo do
                    between two pulses?  And what happens if the pulses stop
                    coming?</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <p>
                As long as the same pulse keeps arriving at the servo, it stays at that one position and is held there.  If you shut the signal down there is no
                power on the motor.  So a servo rotates to the commanded position and then stays there as long as you apply the same pulses.  The signal is a
                position signal, and you have to keep sending it.  That makes this a
                different use of PWM from the motor's: there the duty cycle set an
                average voltage that went to the motor, and here we are using PWM as a way to communicate with the controller rather than sending it to the motor directly.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-between-reveal" instructor="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>As long as the same pulse keeps arriving at the servo, it stays at that one position and is held there. If you shut the signal down there is no power on the motor.</li>
        <li>So a servo rotates to the commanded position and then stays there as long as you apply the same pulses. The signal is a position signal, and you have to keep sending it.</li>
        <li>That makes this a different use of PWM from the motor's: there the duty cycle set an average voltage that went to the motor, and here we are using PWM as a way to communicate with the controller rather than sending it to the motor directly.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>REVEAL, after the activity. Her slide 10 note.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                The controller also has a limit to what it can resolve.  The datasheet for the Tower Pro SG92R lists a <term>dead band width</term> of 1 µs: a change in
                pulse width smaller than that produces no change in the commanded position.  We'll need that number when we choose how to configure our timer to produce the pulses.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-dead-band">
    <ul>
        <li>The controller also has a limit to what it can resolve. The datasheet for the Tower Pro SG92R lists a <term>dead band width</term> of 1 µs: a change in pulse width smaller than that produces no change in the commanded position.</li>
        <li>We'll need that number when we choose how to configure our timer to produce the pulses.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Defined here so that the resolution table can use it without a detour.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                Almost every pin on the Nucleo is capable of PWM, driven by one timer or
                another (<xref ref="fig-servo-pwm-pins"/>).  We look up which timer drives
                a given pin, and which alternate function to select, in the datasheet; the
                pinout in <xref ref="fig-servo-pwm-pins"/> then says where that pin is on
                the header.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-pwm-pins">
                <caption>The Nucleo-C031C6's headers, with each pin marked by what it
                can do.  Almost every pin can carry a PWM output, driven by one timer or
                another; Table 12 of the STM32C031C6 datasheet, <em>Pin assignment and
                description</em>, says which timer and which alternate function for each pin.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/slide14_ef97f387.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-pwm-pins" ref="fig-servo-pwm-pins">
    <caption>Almost every pin can carry PWM. Which timer, and which alternate function, is Table 12 of the STM32C031C6 datasheet, Pin assignment and description; where the pin is on the header is this pinout.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min, then the Table 12 activity and its answer. The wall copy of the pinout is too small to read from the back; the header names come off the pinout on their laptops.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-tim14-pins">
                <title>Which pins can TIM14 drive?</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Which timer a pin can carry, and under which alternate function, is a
                    property of the STM32C031C6 chip, so it is in the chip's
                    <url href="external/stm32c031_datasheet.pdf">datasheet</url>: Table 12,
                    <em>Pin assignment and description</em>, on page 30 names the
                    functions, and the alternate-function tables on pages 36 to 39 give the
                    AF number.  Which chip pin is at which position on the Arduino header is
                    a property of the Nucleo board, and that is the Nucleo pinout.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-pins-find">
                    <statement>
                        <p>TIM14 has one PWM output, its channel 1, named TIM14_CH1 in the datasheet, and the chip can route that output to any of several pins.  Fill in the table: every pin that can carry TIM14_CH1, the alternate
                        function (AF) number it is under, and, if the pin reaches the
                        Arduino header, its name there.  Table 12 of the STM32C031C6
                        datasheet, <em>Pin assignment and description</em>, page 30, names
                        the functions; the alternate-function tables on pages 36 to 39 give
                        the AF number.</p>
                        <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                            <col width="25%"/>
                            <col width="25%"/>
                            <col width="50%"/>
                            <row bottom="major">
                                <cell>Pin</cell>
                                <cell>AF</cell>
                                <cell>Arduino header name, if any</cell>
                            </row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                            <row><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                        </tabular>
                    </statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-pins-choose">
                    <statement><p>One of them carried the motor's PWM in the motor
                    chapter.  Which is it, what is its name on the Arduino header, and
                    what does choosing it save us today?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-tim14-pins">
                <p>Table 12 lists TIM14_CH1 on five pins: PF0, PA4, PA7, PB1 and PA8.  The
                alternate-function tables give the number for each: PA4 and PA7 are AF4,
                PB1 is AF0, PA8 is AF13, and PF0 is AF2.  Neither PA8 nor PF0 reaches the Arduino header (PF0 is an oscillator pin), so the real choice is PA4 (A2), PA7 (D11) and PB1 (A3). PA7 is D11 on the Arduino header, and it is where the motor's PWM
                was, so the pin configuration in the driver, alternate-function mode and
                AF4, is exactly the one we read on Day 11x and nothing about the pin setup
                changes today.  PA4 and PB1 are also A2 and A3 on the analog header, which
                is another reason to leave them alone.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3: 18 min = 2 (callback and 16-bit recall) + 5 (commit 1) + 7 (commit 2) + 4 (commit 3) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15-timer-design">
            <title>Part 3: Designing the Timer for the Servo's Pulse</title>
            <p>
                On Day 11x we worked out what the prescaler and the auto-reload value would
                have to be for a 50 Hz PWM (<xref ref="task-day11x-stretch-50hz"/>): the
                12 MHz clock has to be divided down by a factor of 240,000 to get 50 Hz
                (12,000,000 clock cycles per second divided by 50 periods per second is
                240,000 cycles per period), and the prescaler and the counter split that
                factor between them.  Today we'll repeat that computation for the servo, with a new constraint: the servo only uses pulse widths between 1 ms and 2 ms, and
                its dead band sets the finest change in width it can use.  The numbers we settle here become the five <c>#define</c> lines of the program we'll build afterwards.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-second-design">
    <ul>
        <li>On Day 11x we worked out what the prescaler and the auto-reload value would have to be for a 50 Hz PWM: the 12 MHz clock has to be divided down by a factor of 240,000 (12,000,000 cycles per second divided by 50 periods per second), and the prescaler and the counter split that factor between them.</li>
        <li>Today we'll repeat that computation for the servo, with a new constraint: the servo only uses pulse widths between 1 ms and 2 ms, and its dead band sets the finest change in width it can use.</li>
        </ul>
    <caption>The numbers we settle here become the five #define lines of the program we'll build afterwards.</caption>
    <note>≈ 2 min. The Day 11x callback and today's constraint; the timer recall is the next slide.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                Recall how TIM14 produces the pulse (<xref ref="fig-tim14-block-full"/>):
                the prescaler divides the 12 MHz clock down, the counter counts the divided pulses up to the auto-reload value ARR and
                starts again.  ARR is a 16-bit register, so the largest count it can hold is 65,535.
            </p>
            <p>
                We need a 50 Hz PWM frequency, so one period is 1/(50 Hz) = 20 ms, so the counter needs to take 20 ms to count from 0 to its auto-reload value.
                The counter is clocked from the 12 MHz system clock through the prescaler, so there are many ways to accomplish this.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-timer-recall" ref="fig-tim14-block-full" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>Recall the timer: the prescaler divides the 12 MHz clock down, and the counter counts the divided pulses up to the auto-reload value ARR and starts again. ARR is a 16-bit register, so the largest count it can hold is 65,535.</li>
        <li>We need a 50 Hz PWM frequency, so one period is 1/(50 Hz) = 20 ms, so the counter needs to take 20 ms to count from 0 to its auto-reload value. The counter is clocked from the 12 MHz system clock through the prescaler, so there are many ways to accomplish this.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min, then the prescaler table. Day 11x's TIM14 block diagram, by refPage. The 65,535 is the number the table turns on.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-prescaler-table">
                <title>Which of these are usable?</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>Fill in the table below.  For each prescaler, what does the counter's clock frequency become, and how many counts does it take to get to 20 ms?  Then decide which rows are usable, and why the others are
                    not.</p>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <col width="30%"/>
                        <col width="35%"/>
                        <col width="35%"/>
                        <row bottom="major">
                            <cell>Prescaler</cell>
                            <cell>Timer clock</cell>
                            <cell>Counts for 20 ms (must fit)</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>1</cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>2</cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>6</cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>60</cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>240</cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-prescaler-table">
                <p>Down the rows: prescaler 1 gives a 12 MHz timer clock and 240,000
                counts for 20 ms; 2 gives 6 MHz and 120,000; 6 gives 2 MHz and 40,000;
                60 gives 200 kHz and 4,000; 240 gives 50 kHz and 1,000.  The first two
                rows are unusable: 240,000 and 120,000 do not fit in a 16-bit
                auto-reload register, whose largest value is 65,535.  The other three
                fit, and the next activity chooses between them.</p>
            </instructor>
            <p>
                A 50 Hz frequency gives a pulse period of <m>T_p = 1/(50\text{ Hz}) =
                20\text{ ms}</m>, and the counter divides that period into ARR + 1 bins,
                where ARR is the auto-reload value the counter counts up to
                (<xref ref="fig-servo-pulse-resolution"/>).  So 20 ms/(ARR + 1)
                is the smallest pulse width the timer can produce, <m>T_0</m>, and it
                is also the smallest change in pulse width it can make.  The servo's protocol specifies pulse widths between 1 ms and 2 ms, so the number of steps between 1 ms and 2 ms is the number of distinct positions the timer can command: the resolution.  By convention 1 ms and 2 ms correspond to −90° and +90°, so 180° divided by the number of steps is the angle per step.  Putting a number in: on the table's
                first row the period is divided into 240,000 bins, so <m>T_0</m> would be
                20 ms/240,000, about 83 ns.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-pulse-resolution">
                <caption>The pulse period <m>T_p</m> and the smallest pulse width
                <m>T_0</m>.  The counter divides each period into ARR + 1 bins, so
                <m>T_0 = T_p/(\text{ARR}+1)</m> is both the narrowest pulse the timer can
                make and the step between one pulse width and the next.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/fig-servo-pulse-resolution.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-resolution" ref="fig-servo-pulse-resolution" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>A 50 Hz frequency gives a pulse period of Tₚ = 20 ms, and the counter divides that period into ARR + 1 bins, where ARR is the auto-reload value. So 20 ms/(ARR + 1) is the smallest pulse width the timer can produce, T₀, and it is also the smallest change in pulse width it can make. In the first row of the previous table that would be 20 ms/240,000, about 83 ns.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Her slide 15 note, near verbatim, first half.</note>
</slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-steps-angle">
    <ul>
        <li>The servo's protocol specifies pulse widths between 1 ms and 2 ms, so the number of steps between 1 ms and 2 ms is the number of distinct positions the timer can command: the resolution.</li>
        <li>By convention 1 ms and 2 ms correspond to −90° and +90°, so 180° divided by the number of steps is the angle per step.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min, then the resolution table and its reveal. Her slide 15 note, second half.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-resolution-table">
                <title>Which one do you like best?</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>For the three usable rows, fill in the smallest pulse width, the
                    number of steps between 1 ms and 2 ms, and the angle per step.
                    Then choose a row you want to use in your design and explain why.</p>
                    <tabular halign="center" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                        <col width="16%"/>
                        <col width="16%"/>
                        <col width="24%"/>
                        <col width="22%"/>
                        <col width="22%"/>
                        <row bottom="major">
                            <cell>Prescaler</cell>
                            <cell>ARR + 1</cell>
                            <cell><m>T_0 = 20\text{ ms}/(\text{ARR}+1)</m></cell>
                            <cell>Steps, 1 ms to 2 ms</cell>
                            <cell>Angle per step</cell>
                        </row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>6</cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                        <row bottom="minor"><cell>60</cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                        <row><cell>240</cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell><cell><line>&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                    </tabular>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-resolution-table">
                <p>Lead with the chosen row.  A prescaler of 60 gives a 200 kHz timer
                clock and ARR + 1 = 4000, so <m>T_0</m> = 20 ms/4000 = 5 µs; the 1000 µs
                of pulse range between 1 ms and 2 ms is 1000/5 = 200 steps; and 180°/200
                = 0.9° per step.  That is the one.</p>
                <p>Then we'll look at the two we did not choose.  A prescaler of 6 gives
                ARR + 1 = 40,000, <m>T_0</m> = 0.5 µs, 2000 steps and 0.09° per step.
                Amazing angular resolution!  But this thing is made out of plastic.  So, is
                that worth it?  Each step is half the servo's 1 µs dead band, so the servo
                cannot resolve a single step and the controller acts on every second one at
                best, and a plastic gear train has backlash well beyond what 0.09° would
                buy.  That is more finely grained than is worth doing.  A prescaler of 240
                gives ARR + 1 = 1000, <m>T_0</m> = 20 µs, 50 steps and 3.6° per step, four
                times coarser than the row we chose.  So a prescaler of 60 is great, and
                240 would be fine too.</p>
            </instructor>
            <p>
                Timer 14 will count continuously from 0 to the value stored in
                <c>TIM14_ARR</c>.  It will drive the connected pin HIGH while the counter is
                below the value stored in <c>TIM14_CCR1</c>, and drive the pin LOW while
                the counter is between <c>TIM14_CCR1</c> and <c>TIM14_ARR</c>.  <c>TIM14_ARR</c> and <c>TIM14_CCR1</c> hold counts, not times.  To convert a pulse width to a count, divide the width by the duration of one count, <m>T_0</m>, from your table.  For the 240 row, for example, one count is 20 µs, so a 1 ms pulse is 1000 µs ÷ 20 µs = 50 counts.  Now that you have chosen a row, you can work out the numbers the program needs.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-timer-behaves" ref="fig-tim14-register-map" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>Timer 14 will count continuously from 0 to the value stored in TIM14_ARR. It will drive the connected pin HIGH while the counter is below the value stored in TIM14_CCR1, and drive the pin LOW while the counter is between TIM14_CCR1 and TIM14_ARR.</li>
        <li>TIM14_ARR and TIM14_CCR1 hold counts, not times. To convert a pulse width to a count, divide the width by the duration of one count, T₀, from your table. For the 240 row, for example, one count is 20 µs, so a 1 ms pulse is 1000 µs ÷ 20 µs = 50 counts.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min, then the fill-in and its reveal. Her slide 19's lead, verbatim, over Day 11x's register map (refPage). Say the transition aloud: now that you have chosen a row, you can work out the numbers the program needs.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-fill-in">
                <title>Choosing TIM14_ARR and TIM14_CCR1</title>
                <statement>
                        <p>With your chosen values, complete the table below to help you prepare for your code.  The names in capitals are the <c>#define</c> lines of the program we'll build next.</p>
                        <tabular halign="left" top="major" bottom="major" left="minor" right="minor">
                            <col width="40%"/>
                            <col width="60%"/>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell>The STM32C031C6's system clock runs at</cell><cell><line>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell>The prescaler you chose, <c>PWM_PSC_FACTOR</c></cell><cell><line>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell>The counter's clock frequency after the prescaler</cell><cell><line>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell>ARR + 1, the counts in one 20 ms period, <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c></cell><cell><line>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell>CCR1 for a 1 ms pulse, <c>SERVO_MIN</c></cell><cell><line>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                            <row bottom="minor"><cell>CCR1 for a 1.5 ms pulse, <c>SERVO_MID</c></cell><cell><line>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                            <row><cell>CCR1 for a 2 ms pulse, <c>SERVO_MAX</c></cell><cell><line>&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;</line><line>&#160;</line></cell></row>
                        </tabular>
                    </statement>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-fill-in">
                <p>The system clock runs at 12 MHz; a prescaler of 60 slows it to
                200 kHz; ARR + 1 = 4000 = <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c>.  At 200 kHz one count is
                5 µs, so 1 ms is 200 counts = <c>SERVO_MIN</c>, 1.5 ms is 300 =
                <c>SERVO_MID</c>, and 2 ms is 400 = <c>SERVO_MAX</c>.  Important to know
                <c>SERVO_MIN</c> and <c>SERVO_MAX</c>, since you HAVE to stay between
                those to not damage the servo.</p>
                </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 4: 12 min = 5 (the files, tim.c whole on two slides, and the driver's two changes) + 1 (TIM16) + 2 (the map's one C fact) + 4 (map commit and reveal) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15-program">
            <title>Part 4: Reading the Program</title>
            <p>
                The program is <c>Day15_servo_template.c</c>, and it comes with a timer
                driver in two files, <c>tim.c</c> and <c>tim.h</c>.  It also uses your
                own ADC driver, the <c>adc.c</c> and <c>adc.h</c> you built in Lab 5.
                The template is complete except for the five <c>#define</c> lines whose values you just chose and one line in the loop, the map from the potentiometer's reading to a pulse width.  Here is the header, <c>tim.h</c>, and the three functions it declares:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#ifndef TIM_H_
#define TIM_H_

#include <stdint.h>

void tim16_ms_interrupt_init(int milliseconds);
void tim14_pa7_pwm_init(uint16_t prescaleFactor, uint16_t timerMax);
void tim14_pwm_set(uint16_t pulse);
#endif /* TIM_H_ */
]]></code></program>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-files">
    <p>The program is <c>Day15_servo_template.c</c>, and it comes with a timer driver in two files, <c>tim.c</c> and <c>tim.h</c>. It also uses your own ADC driver, the <c>adc.c</c> and <c>adc.h</c> you built in Lab 5. The template is complete except for the five <c>#define</c> lines whose values you just chose and one line in the loop, the map from the potentiometer's reading to a pulse width. Here is the header, <c>tim.h</c>, and the three functions it declares:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim16_ms_interrupt_init(int milliseconds);
void tim14_pa7_pwm_init(uint16_t prescaleFactor, uint16_t timerMax);
void tim14_pwm_set(uint16_t pulse);
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Three files plus their own adc.c/adc.h; the include guard is left off the slide.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                The TIM14 functions in the timer library, <c>tim.c</c>, are the ones we used on Day 11x (<xref ref="subsec-day11x-driver"/>), with two changes.  The prescale
                factor and the counter's top value are now parameters, passed in from the
                program, rather than <c>#define</c> lines at the top of the driver.  And <c>tim14_pwm_set()</c> no longer checks its value: on Day 11x it checked that the value it was given was within the range the counter can represent before writing it to <c>CCR1</c>; here it writes the value it is given.  The range check is now in the program, in <c>updateServo()</c>, where the range is <c>SERVO_MIN</c> to <c>SERVO_MAX</c>.  Every other register write in <c>tim14_pa7_pwm_init()</c> is the same as before; the whole file is printed in <xref ref="subsec-servo-ref-driver"/>, and <xref ref="fig-tim14-register-map"/> has the registers.  Here are the lines of <c>tim14_pa7_pwm_init()</c> that take the two numbers, and the whole of <c>tim14_pwm_set()</c>.  The initialization writes
                <c>ARR = timerMax-1</c>, so <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c> is the number of counts
                in one period, and <c>tim14_pwm_set()</c> writes <c>CCR1 = value</c>,
                so the value is the number of counts the pin is HIGH, the same convention
                as on Day 11x.  <c>SERVO_MID</c> is the middle of the range; the loop does
                not use it, but it is the number that parks the horn at its center.
            </p>
                        <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim14_pa7_pwm_init(uint16_t prescaleFactor, uint16_t timerMax) {
	// ... the pin and the clock setup, as on Day 11x ...

	// Set prescaler
	TIM14->PSC = prescaleFactor-1;

	// Set autoreload value (counter runs 0..timerMax-1: timerMax counts per period)
	TIM14->ARR = timerMax-1;

	// Set the compare register value - start with a speed of 0
	TIM14->CCR1 = 0;

	// ... the PWM mode, the output enable and the timer enable, as on Day 11x ...
}
]]></code></program>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim14_pwm_set(uint16_t value) {
	// PWM mode 1: pin is HIGH while CNT < CCR1, i.e. for exactly 'value' counts
	TIM14->CCR1 = value;
}
]]></code></program>
            
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void updateServo(uint16_t value) {
	if (value>SERVO_MAX)
		value = SERVO_MAX;
	else if (value < SERVO_MIN)
		value = SERVO_MIN;
	tim14_pwm_set(value);
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                On Day 11x the limit in <c>tim14_pwm_set()</c> was the timer's: the largest count the counter can represent.  The servo's safe range, 1 ms to 2 ms, is much narrower than that, and it is a property of the servo, not of the timer.  Outside that range the servo drives against its own mechanical stops.  So the program, which is written for this servo, applies the limit, in <c>updateServo()</c>, and every value reaches <c>CCR1</c> through that function.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-tim-c-1">
    <p>The whole of <c>tim.c</c>, with its comments left out, in two halves. First the tick timer and the one-line write of <c>CCR1</c>:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "tim.h"
#include "ES28.h"
#define TIM16_PSC_FACTOR	12000          // 12 MHz / 12000 = 1 kHz

void tim16_ms_interrupt_init(int milliseconds) {
	RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM16EN;
	TIM16->PSC = TIM16_PSC_FACTOR - 1;
	TIM16->ARR = milliseconds - 1;
	TIM16->CNT = 0;
	TIM16->DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;
	NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM16_IRQn);
	TIM16->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}

void tim14_pwm_set(uint16_t value) {
	TIM14->CCR1 = value;
}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 1 min. The whole picture before the two changes. The book's Reference section prints the file with its comments.</note>
</slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-tim-c-2">
    <p>And the TIM14 setup, every register write of which is Day 11x's:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// tim.c, second half: tim14_pa7_pwm_init() whole
void tim14_pa7_pwm_init(uint16_t prescaleFactor, uint16_t timerMax) {
	RCC->IOPENR |=  RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;
	GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Msk;
	GPIOA->MODER |= GPIO_ALTERNATE << GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Pos;
	GPIOA->AFR[0] &= ~GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Msk;
	GPIOA->AFR[0] |= GPIO_AF4 << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Pos;
	RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;
	TIM14->PSC = prescaleFactor-1;
	TIM14->ARR = timerMax-1;
	TIM14->CCR1 = 0;
	TIM14->CCMR1 &= ~TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Msk;
	TIM14->CCMR1 |= TIM_OC1_PWM1 << TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Pos;
	TIM14->CCMR1 &= ~TIM_CCMR1_CC1S_Msk;
	TIM14->CCMR1 |= TIM_CC1_OUTPUT << TIM_CCMR1_CC1S_Pos;
	TIM14->CCER |= TIM_CCER_CC1E;
	TIM14->EGR |= TIM_EGR_UG;
	TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Do not re-walk the register writes; the two changes are the next slide.</note>
</slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-driver-numbers">
        <p>The TIM14 functions in the timer library, <c>tim.c</c>, are the ones we used on Day 11x, with two changes. First, the prescale factor and the counter's top value are now parameters of <c>tim14_pa7_pwm_init()</c>, passed in from the program, rather than <c>#define</c> lines at the top of the driver. The initialization writes <c>ARR = timerMax-1</c>, so <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c> is the number of counts in one period, and <c>tim14_pwm_set()</c> writes <c>CCR1 = value</c>, so the value is the number of counts the pin is HIGH, the same convention as on Day 11x. Second, <c>tim14_pwm_set()</c> no longer checks its value against the counter's range: it writes the value it is given.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim14_pa7_pwm_init(uint16_t prescaleFactor, uint16_t timerMax) {
	// ... the pin and the clock setup, as on Day 11x ...
	TIM14->PSC = prescaleFactor-1;
	TIM14->ARR = timerMax-1;      // timerMax counts per period
	TIM14->CCR1 = 0;
	// ... the PWM mode and the enables, as on Day 11x ...
}

void tim14_pwm_set(uint16_t value) {
	TIM14->CCR1 = value;          // HIGH for exactly 'value' counts; no range check
}
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Comments abridged on the slide; the listing in the book carries the file's own. SERVO_MID, which the loop does not use, is the number that parks the horn at its center. Page back to the register map on the What Timer 14 will do slide if anyone wants the registers.</note>
</slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-range-check">
        <p>The range check is now in the program, in <c>updateServo()</c>, and every value reaches <c>CCR1</c> through that function.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void updateServo(uint16_t value) {
	if (value>SERVO_MAX)
		value = SERVO_MAX;
	else if (value < SERVO_MIN)
		value = SERVO_MIN;
	tim14_pwm_set(value);
}
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
                <li>On Day 11x the limit in <c>tim14_pwm_set()</c> was the timer's: the largest count the counter can represent. The servo's safe range, 1 ms to 2 ms, is much narrower than that, and it is a property of the servo, not of the timer. Outside that range the servo drives against its own mechanical stops. So the program, which is written for this servo, applies the limit.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                The program reads the potentiometer on your breadboard (the knob, not the one inside the servo) twice a second.  TIM14 is used to create the pulse, so the timing tick for the potentiometer reading comes from a second timer, TIM16
                (Reference Manual §18, <em>General-purpose timers (TIM16/TIM17)</em>).
                <c>tim16_ms_interrupt_init()</c> is the periodic-interrupt setup from Day 8 (<xref ref="subsec-day8-interrupt-prog"/>) on a different timer, with the period as a parameter; the listing below follows the Day 8 setup line for line.
                TIM16's interrupt is position 21 in the vector table
                (<xref ref="subsec-timers-ref-family"/>), and the startup file's name for
                that slot is <c>TIM16_IRQHandler()</c>.  The handler raises
                <c>timerFlag</c>, and the main loop takes one reading and lowers it
                again.</p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim16_ms_interrupt_init(int milliseconds) {
	/* enable clock access to timer 16 (on APB bus) */
	RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM16EN;

	/* Set prescaler value */
	TIM16->PSC = TIM16_PSC_FACTOR - 1; // starts counting at 0

	/* Set auto-reload value */
	TIM16->ARR = milliseconds - 1;

	/* Clear counter */
	TIM16->CNT = 0;

	/* Enable update interrupt */
	TIM16->DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;

	/* Enable timer interrupt in NVIC */
	NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM16_IRQn);

	/* Enable timer */
	TIM16->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}
]]></code></program>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-tim16">
    <p>The program reads the potentiometer on your breadboard (the knob, not the one inside the servo) twice a second. TIM14 is used to create the pulse, so the timing tick for the potentiometer reading comes from a second timer, TIM16 (Reference Manual §18, <em>General-purpose timers (TIM16/TIM17)</em>). <c>tim16_ms_interrupt_init()</c> is the periodic-interrupt setup from Day 8 on a different timer, with the period as a parameter.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void tim16_ms_interrupt_init(int milliseconds) {
	RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM16EN;   // clock access to timer 16
	TIM16->PSC = TIM16_PSC_FACTOR - 1;     // 12000: a 1 kHz count
	TIM16->ARR = milliseconds - 1;
	TIM16->CNT = 0;
	TIM16->DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;           // update interrupt
	NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM16_IRQn);
	TIM16->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}
]]></code></program>
    <caption>The Day 8 setup, on TIM16.</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Comments abridged on the slide. Day 8 recall; do not re-walk the six steps.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                One line of code remains: the map from a potentiometer reading, 0 to 4095, onto a pulse width between <c>SERVO_MIN</c> and <c>SERVO_MAX</c>.  One C fact to keep in mind when you write it: <c>/</c> on two integers discards the remainder, so the order in which you multiply and divide matters.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-map">
    <ul>
        <li>One line of code remains: the map from a potentiometer reading, 0 to 4095, onto a pulse width between SERVO_MIN and SERVO_MAX.</li>
        <li>One C fact to keep in mind when you write it: <c>/</c> on two integers discards the remainder, so the order in which you multiply and divide matters.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 1 min, then the map activity and its reveal. Do not work an example on the wall: the map is their work.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-map">
                <title>Map the potentiometer onto the pulse</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>Write the expression that maps <c>pot_value</c> onto <c>pwm_value</c>,
                    using the names in the template (<c>MAXADC</c> is 4096).  Then check it by hand: what does it give for readings of 0, 2048 and 4095, and are all three inside the
                    servo's safe range?</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-map">
                <p>The line is
                <c>pwm_value = SERVO_MIN + pot_value*(SERVO_MAX-SERVO_MIN)/MAXADC;</c>.
                A reading of 0 gives 200; 2048 gives 200 + 2048 × 200/4096 = 300; and
                4095 gives 200 + 819,000/4096 = 200 + 199 = 399, so the map never quite
                reaches <c>SERVO_MAX</c>.  That is not a defect; it is what dividing by
                4096 rather than 4095 does, and the top step is one count.</p>
                <p>Two arithmetic notes.  <c>pot_value</c> is a <c>uint16_t</c>, and the product is computed in <c>int</c>, 32 bits, so 4095 × 200 fits easily; the Reference section's integer-scaling note has the detail.
                And <c>updateServo()</c> limits every value the map computes to
                <c>SERVO_MIN</c> through <c>SERVO_MAX</c> before it reaches CCR1, so a slip
                in the map cannot send the servo past its range.  (The initialization is
                the exception: it parks <c>CCR1</c> at 0, so nothing is commanded until the
                first reading.)</p>
            </instructor>
            <p>
                Here is the whole program as you download it.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* servo.c -- Control a servo motor with a potentiometer
 * Potentiometer:  	Analog channel PA0
 * Servo PWM input:	PA7
 */

#include <stdio.h>
#include "ES28.h"
#include "uart.h"
#include "adc.h"
#include "tim.h"

/* Clock runs at 12MHz */
#define PWM_PSC_FACTOR              // prescaler
#define PWM_TIMER_MAX               // 20 ms
#define SERVO_MIN                   // 1 ms
#define SERVO_MID                   // 1.5 ms
#define SERVO_MAX                   // 2 ms

#define MAXADC          4096        // 12-bit converter

volatile uint8_t timerFlag = 0;     // Do not forget the volatile!

void updateServo(uint16_t value);

// Main program
int main(void) {
    uint16_t pot_value = 0;
    uint16_t pwm_value = SERVO_MIN;

    uart2_init();
    tim14_pa7_pwm_init(PWM_PSC_FACTOR, PWM_TIMER_MAX);
    pa0_adc_init();

    printf("Potentiometer-controlled servo\n\r");

    timerFlag = 0;
    tim16_ms_interrupt_init(500);

    while(1) {
        if(timerFlag==1) {
            start_conversion();
            pot_value = adc_read();         // Pot value (0 to MAXADC-1)

/*******************/
            pwm_value =                     // Map pot_value to pwm_value
/*******************/

            updateServo(pwm_value);         // Update the PWM
            printf("pot_value=%d, \tpwm_value=%d\n\r", pot_value, pwm_value);
            timerFlag=0;                    // Put the flag down
        }
        // Processor could sleep here
    }
    return 0;                               // never reached
}
void updateServo(uint16_t value) {
    if (value>SERVO_MAX)
        value = SERVO_MAX;
    else if (value < SERVO_MIN)
        value = SERVO_MIN;
    tim14_pwm_set(value);
}

void TIM16_IRQHandler(void) {
    __disable_irq();
    /* Clear UIF in status register */
    TIM16->SR = ~TIM_SR_UIF;
    timerFlag = 1;
    __enable_irq();
}
]]></code></program>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-template">
    <p>Here is the program as you download it: the defines and the setup first. The five <c>#define</c> values are blank. Fill in the numbers you chose.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define PWM_PSC_FACTOR              // prescaler
#define PWM_TIMER_MAX               // 20 ms
#define SERVO_MIN                   // 1 ms
#define SERVO_MID                   // 1.5 ms
#define SERVO_MAX                   // 2 ms
#define MAXADC          4096        // 12-bit converter

    uint16_t pot_value = 0;
    uint16_t pwm_value = SERVO_MIN;

    uart2_init();
    tim14_pa7_pwm_init(PWM_PSC_FACTOR, PWM_TIMER_MAX);
    pa0_adc_init();
    timerFlag = 0;
    tim16_ms_interrupt_init(500);
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 1 min. The defines are the five blanks; the three init calls are where they go.</note>
</slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-template-loop">
    <p>And the loop, with its one blank.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
    while(1) {
        if(timerFlag==1) {
            start_conversion();
            pot_value = adc_read();         // Pot value (0 to MAXADC-1)

/*******************/
            pwm_value =                     // Map pot_value to pwm_value
/*******************/

            updateServo(pwm_value);         // Update the PWM
            printf("pot_value=%d, \tpwm_value=%d\n\r", pot_value, pwm_value);
            timerFlag=0;                    // Put the flag down
        }
    }
]]></code></program>
    <note>≈ 1 min. The whole file, with its header and the handler, is in the book and on Canvas.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 5: 49 min = 5 (project) + 8 (fill the blanks) + 4 (wire) + 5 (predict) + 22 (build, run, capture; checkpoint at about minute 78, mid-beat) + 5 (center the knob, finish demos) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15-build">
            <title>Part 5: Design Exercise, Verifying the Pulse on the Oscilloscope</title>
            <p>
                We'll verify the pulse on the oscilloscope before the servo is ever attached: a wrong pulse would drive the servo against its mechanical stops, so the pulse is checked on the screen first.  The potentiometer wiring is the ADC chapter's test circuit
                (<xref ref="fig-adc-test-circuit"/>), and the two captures below
                (<xref ref="fig-servo-scope-1ms"/>, <xref ref="fig-servo-scope-2ms"/>)
                are what a good capture looks like.  For the stretch, <c>milliseconds()</c> is explained in <xref ref="subsec-day12-main-loop"/>.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-safety">
    <ul>
        <li>We'll verify the pulse on the oscilloscope before the servo is ever attached: a wrong pulse would drive the servo against its mechanical stops, so the pulse is checked on the screen first.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 30 s, then the build activity, which is the rest of the Part (51 min in all). Page back to the fill-in reveal if anyone asks for the numbers.</note>
</slide>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-build">
                <title>A potentiometer-controlled pulse on PA7</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Work through these in order.  The servo stays unplugged until tomorrow.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-build-project">
                    <statement><p>Copy your <c>TemplateProject</c> to a new project
                    named <c>Servo</c>.  Download <c>Day15_servo_template.c</c>,
                    <c>tim.c</c> and <c>tim.h</c> from Canvas; the two <c>.c</c> files
                    go into <c>Src</c> and <c>tim.h</c> into <c>Inc</c>.  You'll also
                    need your ADC code.  If you have previously added your <c>adc.c</c>
                    and <c>adc.h</c> from Lab 5 to the <c>mylib</c> folder you are good
                    to go; otherwise copy <c>adc.c</c> into <c>Src</c> and <c>adc.h</c>
                    into <c>Inc</c>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-build-blanks">
                    <statement><p>Complete the five <c>#define</c> lines with the numbers
                    you chose earlier today (the prescaler factor, the counter's top value,
                    and the three compare values for a 1 ms, a 1.5 ms and a 2 ms pulse) and
                    the map line.  If your ADC functions have different names from
                    <c>pa0_adc_init()</c>, <c>start_conversion()</c> and <c>adc_read()</c>,
                    adjust those three calls to your own names.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-build-wire">
                    <statement><p>Wire the potentiometer to A0 (PA0) exactly as in the ADC chapter's test circuit: the outer terminals to
                    3.3 V and GND, the wiper to A0.  Connect the AD2's channel 1, the
                    orange lead, to PA7 (D11), and channel 1's ground lead, the one with
                    the white stripe, to a GND pin.  Nothing else is connected to
                    PA7.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15-measure">
                <title>Predict, then measure the pulse</title>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-build-predict">
                    <statement><p>Before you capture anything, think about three things: the time base that shows one full 20 ms period on the
                    screen; the trigger you will use; and the <c>pwm_value</c> your
                    program will print with the knob at one end, in the middle, and at
                    the other end.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-build-run">
                    <statement><p>Build, run, and open CoolTerm.  Turn the knob from one end to the other: <c>pot_value</c> should run from 0 to 4095, and <c>pwm_value</c> between the numbers you predicted.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-build-scope">
                    <statement><p>On the oscilloscope, trigger on the rising edge and measure the pulse width and the period at each end of the knob, and verify that the pulse width sweeps smoothly from 1 ms to 2 ms as you rotate the potentiometer.  Check what you measure against the prediction you made.  The two captures below show what a good capture looks like: note their time base and their trigger setting.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-build-center">
                    <statement><p>Find the knob position that gives a 1.5 ms pulse.  Remove and reattach the knob, if necessary, so that it is at the midway position.  Then show your sweeping pulse to an instructor.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-scope-1ms">
                <caption>The pulse on PA7 with the knob at one end, captured on the AD2 at 5 ms/div with a rising-edge trigger on channel 1 (the empty lower half of the screen is cropped out).  The cursors
                measure the pulse width at 1.0056 ms and the period at 19.91 ms, which
                the cursor table reports as 50.2 Hz.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/fig-servo-scope-1ms.png" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-scope-2ms">
                <caption>The same capture with the knob at the other end.  The pulse is now 2.008 ms wide, and the period has not moved: 19.93 ms against 19.91 ms, 20 ms to within the cursors' placement.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/fig-servo-scope-2ms.png" width="100%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <term>Nothing on the screen, or not what you predicted?</term>  Work down
                this list, since each symptom points somewhere different.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li><term>The project will not build.</term>  Look at the <c>#define</c>
                lines first: a blank one puts nothing where the compiler expects a number,
                and the error is reported at the line that uses the name rather than at the
                blank itself.  Then check that <c>tim.c</c> and your <c>adc.c</c> are in the
                project, and that there is exactly one <c>main()</c> in it.</li>
                <li><term>No pulse at all.</term>  Look at the terminal first.  If
                <c>pot_value</c> is printing, the program is running and the fault is on
                the scope side: channel 1 on PA7 (D11), and its striped ground lead on a
                GND pin.  If nothing prints, the build never reached the board, which is
                the first item's checks.</li>
                <li><term>A pulse of the wrong width or period.</term>  One of the
                <c>#define</c> values is off.  <c>PWM_PSC_FACTOR</c> and
                <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c> set the period; <c>SERVO_MIN</c> and <c>SERVO_MAX</c>
                set the width at the two ends of the knob.</li>
                <li><term>A pulse that does not move with the knob.</term> The fault is in the ADC or the map. Look at <c>pot_value</c> in the printout: if it is not changing, the fault is before the map; if it is changing and <c>pwm_value</c> is not, the fault is in the map.</li>
            </ol>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-capture-1ms" ref="fig-servo-scope-1ms">
    <caption>The knob at one end: 5 ms/div, rising-edge trigger on channel 1, the cursors on the pulse width and the period.</caption>
    <note>Reference for the time base and trigger, not the answer: their own predicted numbers come first.</note>
</slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-capture-2ms" ref="fig-servo-scope-2ms">
    <caption>The knob at the other end: the pulse is wider and the period has not moved.</caption>
</slide>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-symptoms">
    <p>Nothing on the screen, or not what you predicted? Work down this list, since each symptom points somewhere different.</p>
    <ol>
        <li><term>The project will not build.</term> Look at the <c>#define</c> lines first: a blank one puts nothing where the compiler expects a number, and the error is reported at the line that uses the name rather than at the blank itself. Then check that <c>tim.c</c> and your <c>adc.c</c> are in the project, and that there is exactly one <c>main()</c>.</li>
        <li><term>No pulse at all.</term>  Look at the terminal first.  If
                <c>pot_value</c> is printing, the program is running and the fault is on
                the scope side: channel 1 on PA7 (D11), and its striped ground lead on a
                GND pin.  If nothing prints, the build never reached the board, which is
                the first item's checks.</li>
        <li><term>A pulse of the wrong width or period.</term> One of the <c>#define</c> values is off. <c>PWM_PSC_FACTOR</c> and <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c> set the period; <c>SERVO_MIN</c> and <c>SERVO_MAX</c> set the width at the two ends of the knob.</li>
        <li><term>A pulse that does not move with the knob.</term> The fault is in the ADC or the map. If <c>pot_value</c> is not changing in the printout, the fault is before the map; if it is and <c>pwm_value</c> is not, the fault is in the map.</li>
    </ol>
    <note>Leave this up during the build. Checkpoint about 30 minutes into the build: the triage list with the causes is the instructor block that follows.</note>
</slide>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-template-complete">
                <p>The completed template.  It differs from the download in exactly two places: the five <c>#define</c> values and the map line.</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* Clock runs at 12MHz */
#define PWM_PSC_FACTOR  60          // prescaler: 12 MHz / 60 = 200 kHz
#define PWM_TIMER_MAX   4000        // 20 ms: 200 kHz / 4000 = 50 Hz
#define SERVO_MIN       200         // 1 ms
#define SERVO_MID       300         // 1.5 ms
#define SERVO_MAX       400         // 2 ms

#define MAXADC          4096        // 12-bit converter

    ...

            start_conversion();
            pot_value = adc_read();         // Pot value (0 to MAXADC-1)

            pwm_value = SERVO_MIN + pot_value*(SERVO_MAX-SERVO_MIN)/MAXADC;

            updateServo(pwm_value);         // Update the PWM
]]></code></program>
                <p>Have each student demonstrate the sweeping pulse and say, without looking
                at their table, why they picked their prescaler and what CCR1 value
                <c>SERVO_MID</c> produces.  A student may go on to the stretch as soon as their own capture matches the reference captures.  The time-base answer: 5 ms/div puts a
                whole period on the screen (her captures); 1 ms/div shows the pulse
                but not the period.  Trigger: rising edge on channel 1.</p>
            </instructor>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-checkpoint">
                <p>This exercise takes students a LONG time; lots of troubleshooting to do.
                Checkpoint about 30 minutes into the build, whatever the clock says (about
                minute 78).  The x-hour is the overflow by design: a student still here at
                the end of class finishes tomorrow, before wiring the servo.  Part 6's first
                beat and the close still fit at minute 100; the wiring itself is
                Wednesday's.  Point a stuck student at the symptom list in the text first;
                the causes below are the part that needs you.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>A pulse that does not move with the knob, and <c>pot_value</c> not changing: the wiper is not on A0, or the ADC calls are not their own.</li>
                    <li><c>pot_value</c> changing and <c>pwm_value</c> stuck at <c>SERVO_MIN</c>: the map divides before it multiplies.</li>
                    <li>A pulse of the wrong width or period: read the five <c>#define</c> values off their screen against the fill-in reveal.</li>
                </ul>
            </instructor>

            <activity xml:id="act-day15-stretch">
                <title>Going further</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>These are optional, for when your pulse is verified.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-stretch-millis">
                    <statement><p>Replace the TIM16 interrupt with <c>milliseconds()</c> from Day 12, the way the motor chapter's main loop uses it: rewrite the loop so that
                    the sampling interval is a <c>#define</c> and no interrupt of your own is needed.  Nothing else about the program changes.  In Lab 8 you are
                    asked to keep track of the sampling interval this way.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15-stretch-finer">
                    <statement><p>The map turns 4096 potentiometer readings into 200 timer
                    counts.  How many readings share one timer count, and what does that
                    mean for how the knob feels as you turn it?  Then: if you wanted one
                    timer count per reading, what would <c>PWM_TIMER_MAX</c> have to be, and
                    what would go wrong with the pulse period?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15-stretch">
                <p><term>The millisecond tick.</term>  Keep the time of the last
                reading and compare against it, the way the Day 12 main loop does:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define SAMPLE_MS	500

  uint32_t last_sample = milliseconds();

  while(1) {
	if (milliseconds() - last_sample >= SAMPLE_MS) {
		last_sample = milliseconds();
		start_conversion();
		pot_value = adc_read();
		pwm_value = SERVO_MIN + pot_value*(SERVO_MAX-SERVO_MIN)/MAXADC;
		updateServo(pwm_value);
		printf("pot_value=%d, \tpwm_value=%d\n\r", pot_value, pwm_value);
	}
  }
]]></code></program>
                <p>The <c>tim16_ms_interrupt_init(500)</c> call, the flag and the
                handler come out.  This is the shape Lab 8 asks for, with
                <c>T</c> as the <c>#define</c>.</p>
                <p><term>One count per reading.</term>  4096/200 is about 20 readings to a
                count, so roughly 20 codes of knob travel go by before the horn moves, which
                is what a student who turns the knob slowly and watches <c>pwm_value</c>
                will notice.  For one count per reading you would need 4096 counts across
                the 1 ms window, so <m>T_0</m> = 244 ns and ARR + 1 for a 20 ms period would
                be 81,920, which a 16-bit register cannot hold.  And 244 ns is far below the
                servo's 1 µs dead band, so the servo could not act on a single step even if
                the register could.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 6: 7 min = 4 (the why, the regulator, the drawing) + 3 (the leads and the order) -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15-power">
            <title>Part 6: Powering the Servo</title>
            <p>
                The reading said the servo is powered from a separate supply and never from
                the Nucleo.  Here is the reason: the servo may draw about
                250 mA while moving, and much more if it is stalled, while your laptop's
                USB port supplies at most 500 mA for the whole board and everything on it.
                High current draw by the servo can starve the Nucleo, causing it to brown
                out and reset itself.  A GPIO pin is worse still: the STM32C031C6 datasheet
                rates any single I/O pin for at most 20 mA, so a servo's power lead on a
                pin would exceed that rating many times over.  So we power it from the same regulator board we used for the motor (<xref ref="fig-tb6612-regulator"/>): the servo's power lead goes to the board's 5V pin, and never to its Vin pin.  Vin is the regulator's input, the adapter's raw, unregulated voltage, up to 12 V, and a servo rated for 4.8 to 6 V would be destroyed there.  <xref ref="fig-servo-powering"/> shows how the servo connects to the board.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-why-separate" ref="fig-tb6612-regulator">
    <ul>
        <li>Why the servo is powered from a separate supply and never from the Nucleo: the servo may draw about 250 mA while moving, and much more if it is stalled, while your laptop's USB port supplies at most 500 mA for the whole board and everything on it.</li>
        <li>High current draw by the servo can starve the Nucleo, causing it to brown out and reset itself. A GPIO pin is worse still: the STM32C031C6 datasheet rates any single I/O pin for at most 20 mA, so a servo's power lead on a pin would exceed that rating many times over.</li>
        <li>So we power it from the same regulator board we used for the motor: the servo's power lead goes to the board's 5V pin, never to Vin. Vin is the adapter's raw, unregulated input, up to 12 V, and the servo is rated for 4.8 to 6 V.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 3 min. Her slide 28; the regulator picture is Day 11's (refPage).</note>
</slide>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-powering">
                <caption>How the servo is powered.  The regulator board is plugged into the breadboard, so each of its pins is reached through the row it sits in.  The servo's red lead, the middle one, goes to the row of the board's 5V pin, and never to Vin.  Its brown lead goes to the breadboard's ground rail, and so does a wire from the board's GND pin, so the servo, the board and the Nucleo, whose GND also feeds that rail, share one ground.  The signal lead, orange on the servo and drawn yellow here, goes to D11 (PA7).  The Nucleo's 3.3 V feeds the other rail, and nothing on the Nucleo powers the servo.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/towerProPowering.png" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-powering" ref="fig-servo-powering">
    <caption>Red, the middle lead, to the row of the regulator board's 5V pin, never Vin. Brown to the ground rail, which the board's GND pin and the Nucleo's GND also feed. Orange to D11 (PA7).</caption>
    <note>≈ 1 min. Her drawing. Say the three connections in words rather than pointing: the rows and rails are on the screen at each table tomorrow, and the wiring is theirs to reason out.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                On the Tower Pro SG92R in your kit the three leads are brown, red and orange (the signal lead is called orange and photographs yellow, <xref ref="fig-servo-leads"/>), and <xref ref="fig-servo-powering"/> shows where each one goes.  We'll do the wiring tomorrow.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-leads">
                <caption>The SG92R on its stand, with the solar tracker's arm bolted to its horn. The three leads leave at the right.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/slide09_a204d4b1.jpg" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day15-leads" ref="fig-servo-leads">
    <ul>
        <li>This is the Tower Pro SG92R in your kit, on its stand with the solar tracker's arm bolted to the horn. Its three leads leave at the right; the signal lead is called orange and photographs yellow.</li>
        <li>We'll do the wiring tomorrow.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>≈ 3 min. The photo is hers. Her slide 27's UNPLUG POWER rule and the power-up order are Day 15x's, where the wiring happens.</note>
</slide>
            <p>
                Tomorrow we'll connect the servo to its 5 V and watch it follow the
                potentiometer.  On Thursday we'll replace the potentiometer with two photocells and start
                on the solar tracker.  Thursday's reading is about the photocell.
            </p>
            <!-- Close, 5 min.  Presenter note for the deck (delivery 2): bring the
                 servo, the regulator board and its adapter, and your multimeter
                 tomorrow.  No homework is due Thursday (Petra, 2026-09-03). -->
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <!-- Day 15x In-Class.  Wednesday x-hour, 50 min, no reading (plans/day15x.md;
         Gate 1 applied and re-checked 2026-09-05 against the passed Day 15).
         Budget, for the deck (delivery 2): settle 2, announcements 1, Part 1 = 3
         (1+2), Part 2 = 34 (4+3+10+5+7+5, the checkpoint inside the 7-minute
         follow beat at about minute 34), Part 3 = 5 (3+2), close 5.  Total 50.
         The UNPLUG-before-rewiring rule and the power-up order (USB first, then
         the adapter) are stated HERE, not on Day 15 (Petra, 2026-09-05).
         OWES (delivery 2): refPage recalls of sl-day15-powering and sl-day15-leads
         (Part 1), of fig-adc-test-circuit (Part 2) and of fig-servo-pulses at
         task-day15x-observe-center (the 180° convention); the close's presenter note
         (bring the tracker fixture, the photocells and the alligator clips).
         Cut order if Part 2 overruns: Part 3 to one sentence in the close, then
         the close to two sentences.  Never cut Part 2. -->
    <section xml:id="sec-servo-day15x">
        <title>Day 15x In-Class: The Servo on Its Own Supply</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we'll connect the servo and watch it follow the potentiometer.
                Its power comes from the regulator board's 5V pin, its ground is shared
                with the Nucleo, and its signal lead goes to PA7 (D11).  Yesterday we
                designed its command pulse and checked it on the AD2 oscilloscope with
                no servo attached, and today we'll put the servo on the end of that
                signal.  The program does not change: it is the
                <c>Day15_servo_template.c</c> you completed yesterday, and it is still
                on your Nucleo, so you should see the servo rotate, smoothly, as you
                adjust the potentiometer.  At the end we'll recall how your ADC library
                reads two channels, and on Thursday we'll use it to read two
                photocells.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- Part 1: 3 min = 1 (the pointer to Tuesday's figure) + 2 (the two rules).
             The lead map is Day 15 Part 6's, by xref here and by refPage in the deck;
             it is stated in full once in this chapter (fig-servo-powering), not here. -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15x-rules">
            <title>Part 1: Wiring the Servo and Powering It Up</title>
            <p>
                <xref ref="fig-servo-powering"/> shows where each of the servo's three
                leads goes.  The signal lead goes to D11 (PA7), the pin TIM14 drives.
            </p>
            <p>
                Unplug power before making any changes to wiring: no USB cable in the
                Nucleo and no adapter in the regulator board while a wire is being
                moved.  The reason is that a wire which lands on the wrong row while the
                5 V supply is live connects that supply to whatever else is in the row.
                On a GPIO pin that is an overvoltage the pin cannot survive; on Vin or
                on ground the outcome depends on the part, and either way you find out
                only after it is done.  With nothing powered, a wire in the wrong row is
                only a wrong wire, and it can be corrected when your wiring is checked.
            </p>
            <p>
                Whenever the servo is connected, the USB cable goes in first and then
                the adapter, as we did for the motor.  With USB connected first the
                STM32C031C6 is running and PA7 is being driven LOW by TIM14, whose
                compare value is 0 until the first reading, before the servo has any
                power, so the servo's signal lead is never left floating while its
                supply is live.  If it were not, the voltage on that lead would not be
                defined, and the servo's controller might respond to it unpredictably.
                We remove power in the opposite order: the adapter out first, then the
                USB cable.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 2: 34 min = 4 (the board and its ground) + 3 (the 5 V check, adapter
             out) + 10 (the pulse re-verified by everyone; a student still in Tuesday's
             exercise finishes it here) + 5 (the three leads, the wiring check) + 7
             (USB, then the adapter; the servo follows the knob; the checkpoint inside
             this beat at about minute 34) + 5 (the observations).
             Her drawing runs the signal lead straight to D11, so the AD2 leaves the
             signal when the servo goes on; no breadboard row is invented for it. -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15x-build">
            <title>Part 2: Design Exercise, the Servo Follows the Potentiometer</title>
            <p>
                If you didn't finish Tuesday's design exercise you need to complete
                that first, before moving on to the next exercise.
                <xref ref="fig-servo-pot-wiring"/> is today's circuit.  The potentiometer
                is where it was yesterday, on A0 (PA0), and the servo is wired as in
                <xref ref="fig-servo-powering"/>.  We'll build it in the order of the
                motor chapter's build-order table (<xref ref="table-day12-build-order"/>):
                we'll measure the regulator board's 5 V first, then check the pulse on
                PA7 again, and connect the servo's leads last.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-servo-pot-wiring">
                <caption>Today's circuit: yesterday's servo wiring with the
                potentiometer on A0 (PA0), its wiper on the rightmost pin of the AIN
                header, and one ground shared by the servo, the regulator board, the
                potentiometer and the Nucleo.  The servo's brown lead is drawn black
                here, and its orange signal lead is drawn yellow.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day15-Servos/towerProPot.png" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15x-wire">
                <title>Wire the servo and bring it up</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Work through these in order.  Nothing is powered while a wire is
                    moved.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day15x-board">
                    <statement><p>With nothing powered, plug the regulator board into the
                    breadboard, and run a wire from the row of its GND pin to the ground
                    rail, the rail the Nucleo's GND already feeds.  Leave the row of its
                    5V pin empty for now.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15x-5v">
                    <statement><p>Plug the adapter into the regulator board, and nothing
                    else.  With the multimeter on DC volts, the black probe on the ground
                    rail and the red probe in the row of the board's 5V pin, you should
                    read 5 V.  The black probe goes on the rail, not on the board's own
                    GND pin, so that the wire you just ran is tested too.  We measure
                    before the servo is connected.  A reading of 0 V means nothing is
                    reaching the board, so check the adapter and how it is seated.  A
                    reading near the adapter's own voltage means the 5V and Vin rows are
                    swapped or the regulator has failed, and the servo does not go on
                    until that is resolved.  Then unplug the adapter.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15x-pulse">
                    <statement><p>Connect the Nucleo over USB, with the AD2's channel 1
                    on PA7 (D11) as it was yesterday, and turn the knob from one end to
                    the other: the pulse still sweeps from 1 ms to 2 ms and the period is
                    still 20 ms.  Everyone does this, whether or not the pulse was
                    verified yesterday.  If it does not, work down Tuesday's symptom list
                    and finish that exercise here, before any servo lead goes on.  Then
                    disconnect the USB cable.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15x-leads">
                    <statement><p>With nothing powered, connect the servo's three leads:
                    the brown lead to the ground rail, the red center lead into the row of
                    the board's 5V pin, not its GND or Vin pin, and the orange signal
                    lead to D11 (PA7).  Then check your wiring with an
                    instructor.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15x-power">
                    <statement><p>Connect the USB cable first, then plug in the adapter.
                    Turn the knob, and you should see the servo rotate, smoothly, as you
                    adjust the potentiometer.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                <term>The servo does not do what you expect?</term>  Work down this
                list, since each symptom points somewhere different.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li><term>The servo does not move at all, and the pulse was
                verified.</term>  The fault is in power or ground before it is in the
                signal.  Measure the row of the board's 5V pin again, with the servo
                connected; check the wire from the board's GND pin to the ground rail,
                and check that the red lead is in the 5V row.  Then the signal: the
                orange lead on D11 (PA7).  If all of that is right and the arm still
                does not move, say so when your wiring is checked; the servo itself may
                have been damaged.</li>
                <li><term>The servo twitches, or the terminal's printout starts over
                from its first line.</term>  The Nucleo is resetting.  The restarting
                printout is the proof of the reset; a twitch on its own is also
                consistent with supply noise.  The servo is drawing its current from the
                Nucleo: its red lead is on the 3.3 V rail or on a Nucleo pin instead of
                the row of the regulator board's 5V pin.  Remove power, the adapter
                first and then the USB cable, and move the lead.</li>
                <li><term>The servo moves but does not follow the knob, or holds one
                end.</term>  Look at the terminal.  If <c>pot_value</c> is not
                changing, the wiper has come off A0 or the potentiometer's outer legs are
                off their rails.  If <c>pot_value</c> is changing and the servo is not
                following it, the program on the board is not yesterday's completed
                template; build and flash it again.</li>
                <li><term>The servo buzzes at one end of the knob's travel.</term>  That
                servo's travel is shorter than the convention's, and it is holding
                against its own stop.  Turn the knob a little toward the center.</li>
            </ol>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15x-checkpoint">
                <p>Checkpoint at about minute 34, inside the follow beat and before the
                observations.  This x-hour exists so that Tuesday's exercise gets
                finished: a student still in Tuesday's template works down the Day 15
                checkpoint list (<c>inst-day15-checkpoint</c>), and wires the servo when
                the pulse is verified, not before.  For everyone else the symptom list
                in the text is the first stop; the causes below are the part that needs
                you.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>No motion, pulse verified, 5 V present at the row: the ground.
                    If the wire from the board's GND pin to the rail is missing, the
                    meter still reads 5 V when its black probe is on the board's own GND
                    pin; the task puts the black probe on the rail so that this wire is
                    tested too.  Repeat the measurement that way.</li>
                    <li>No motion and the red lead is one pin over, on GND (no motion,
                    no damage), or two over, on Vin: on Vin the servo has the adapter's
                    raw input voltage on it.  Adapter out at once, then move the lead;
                    the servo may have been damaged.</li>
                    <li>Twitching and a restarting printout: the brown out from
                    Tuesday's Part 6, the red lead on the Nucleo's 3.3 V or 5V pin.
                    Power down in order (adapter, then USB), move the lead, power up in
                    order.</li>
                    <li>Motion that ignores the knob with <c>pot_value</c> changing: the
                    board is running something other than the completed template, for
                    example if the flash went to a different project.  Build and flash
                    again from the <c>Servo</c> project.</li>
                </ul>
            </instructor>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15x-observe">
                <title>What the servo does</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>With the servo following the knob, look at three things, and write
                    a sentence about each.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day15x-observe-center">
                    <statement><p>Yesterday you found the knob position that gives a
                    1.5 ms pulse and set the knob there.  Is the arm at the middle of its
                    travel at that position?  Then turn the knob to each end: estimate
                    how far the arm turns in all, and compare that with the 180° between
                    1 ms and 2 ms in the convention from the reading.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15x-observe-steps">
                    <statement><p>Turn the knob as slowly as you can while watching
                    <c>pwm_value</c> in the terminal.  Does the arm move continuously or
                    in steps, and what is the smallest change in <c>pwm_value</c> that
                    moves it?  Relate what you see to the smallest step in pulse width
                    your design produces, and to how many potentiometer readings map onto
                    one timer count.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15x-observe-scope">
                    <statement><p>The AD2's channel 1 came off D11 when the servo's signal
                    lead went on.  Say which supply the servo is loading, and whether it
                    draws any appreciable current from the signal pin.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15x-observe">
                <p>The center: at the 1.5 ms knob position the arm is at or near the
                middle of its travel, and how near depends on the servo.  The travel:
                less than 180°, and different from one servo to the next; the reading
                said the endpoints are the convention and that our servos do not go that
                far.  The steps: the arm moves in steps of one timer count, 5 µs of pulse
                width and 0.9° by the design's arithmetic on a servo that turns the full
                180°, and less on a shorter-travel servo; and since 4096 readings map
                onto 200 counts, about 20 codes of <c>pot_value</c> go by before
                <c>pwm_value</c> changes, which is why a very slow turn of the knob
                moves the arm in visible steps.  The signal: the servo loads the
                regulator's 5 V supply, not the signal; the signal lead is an input to
                the servo's controller and draws well under the pin's 20 mA rating,
                while the servo draws about 250 mA from the 5 V lead while it
                moves.</p>
            </instructor>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15x-stretch">
                <title>Going further</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>Optional, for when your servo follows the knob.</p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-day15x-stretch-supply">
                    <statement><p>Move the AD2's channel 1 lead from D11 to the row of
                    the regulator board's 5V pin, with its striped ground lead on the
                    ground rail.  Predict what the 5 V trace will do while the servo is
                    moving and while it is holding still.  Then sweep the knob and
                    look.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-day15x-stretch-hold">
                    <statement><p>Stop turning the knob.  The pulse is still there, and
                    the arm holds.  From what we said on Tuesday about the command
                    signal, what would the arm do if the pulses stopped while the 5 V
                    stayed on, and how would you show that without breaking the
                    power-down order?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15x-stretch">
                <p>The supply: while the servo moves, its current rises and the 5 V
                trace dips, by an amount that depends on the adapter and the regulator,
                and it recovers when the arm arrives and holds.  This is the current
                draw that Tuesday's Part 6 gave as the reason for the separate supply,
                made visible.  The hold: with no pulses there is no power on the motor
                (Tuesday's Part 2: if you shut the signal down there is no power on the
                motor), so the arm is no longer held at its position.  To show it
                without unplugging the USB cable while the adapter is in, stop the
                pulses in software: in PWM mode 1 a compare value of 0 keeps PA7 LOW for
                the whole period, so <c>tim14_pwm_set(0)</c> is called directly in
                place of <c>updateServo(pwm_value)</c> when the knob is at one end (for
                example <c>if (pot_value &lt; 100)</c>), since <c>updateServo()</c>
                would limit 0 up to <c>SERVO_MIN</c>.  The arm goes free at that end of
                the knob and returns to the commanded position as soon as the knob
                leaves it.  Do not have students push on the arm to find out; with no
                pulse the servo does not resist, and that is visible from the horn.</p>
            </instructor>
        </subsection>

        <!-- Part 3: 5 min = 3 (the recall and Table 12) + 2 (the plan on paper). -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day15x-two-channels">
            <title>Part 3: Reading Two ADC Channels, from Lab 5</title>
            <p>
                On Thursday the potentiometer comes out and two photocells go onto the
                arm of this servo, each in a voltage divider whose output is a voltage on
                an analog pin: A0 (PA0) for one and A1 (PA1) for the other.  You have
                already written a program that reads two channels.  In Lab 5 you added
                a function to your ADC library that selects the channel, and you used it
                to move between the potentiometer and the temperature sensor.  To read
                two channels in every sampling interval (each time the flag is raised)
                we call that function twice: select one channel and convert, then
                select the other and convert.  The ADC channel number of each analog pin
                comes from Table 12 of the
                <url href="external/stm32c031_datasheet.pdf">STM32C031C6 datasheet</url>,
                <em>Pin assignment and description</em>, as it did in the ADC chapter:
                A0 is PA0, ADC_IN0, and A1 is PA1, ADC_IN1.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-day15x-two-channels">
                <title>Two channels in yesterday's loop</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>Nothing here needs a working servo.  On paper, with your Lab 5
                    <c>adc.c</c> open, change the body of yesterday's loop, the part
                    inside <c>if(timerFlag==1)</c>, so that every time the flag is raised
                    it reads A0 and then A1.  Which of your functions are called, in what
                    order, and which variable holds each reading?  There is nothing to
                    build today; on Thursday we'll give this loop its two
                    photocells.</p>
                </statement>
            </activity>
            <instructor xml:id="inst-day15x-two-channels">
                <p>With the Lab 5 prototypes (names vary by student): after
                <c>timerFlag</c> is seen set, <c>adc_setChannel(0); v0 =
                adc_getValue();</c> then <c>adc_setChannel(1); v1 =
                adc_getValue();</c>, then the flag goes down.  The points to draw out:
                each conversion follows its own channel selection, so the order is
                select, convert, select, convert and not select, select, convert,
                convert; the channel numbers are the ADC's, 0 and 1, and not the pin
                numbers, which coincide on A0 and A1 but not in general (A3 is PB1 and
                ADC_IN18, as in the ADC chapter's homework); and the two readings are
                the two inputs to the loop we'll design on Thursday, so they are kept in
                two variables rather than printed and discarded.  A student whose
                library selects the channel inside its read function makes two calls
                instead of four; that is fine.  One caution for a library that writes
                CHSELR and starts a conversion at once: the Reference Manual, §14,
                requires waiting for CCRDY after writing CHSELR and before ADSTART, and
                a read function that does not wait can return the previous channel's
                value with no error.  If a student's two readings track each other,
                look here.</p>
            </instructor>
            <p>
                On Thursday we'll put two photocells on the arm of this servo and start
                the solar tracker, so leave the servo wired.  Thursday's reading is the
                photocell and its datasheet.
            </p>
            <!-- Close, 5 min.  Presenter note for the deck (delivery 2): bring the
                 tracker fixture, the photocells and the alligator clips from your kit
                 tomorrow. -->
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-servo-reference">
        <title>Reference: The Servo Command, the Timer Arithmetic, and the Driver</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Lookup material for the servo.  Nothing here is new; it is the same
                information arranged for a quick look-up.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-servo-ref-command">
            <title>The Command Pulse</title>
            <p>
                A hobby servo is commanded by a pulse repeated about every 20 ms
                (50 Hz).  The width of the HIGH pulse is the position command, and the
                servo holds the position only as long as the pulses keep coming; with
                no signal there is no power on the motor.  The convention:
            </p>
            <tabular halign="left">
                <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                    <cell>Pulse width</cell>
                    <cell>Position</cell>
                </row>
                <row><cell>1 ms</cell><cell>−90°, one end of the travel</cell></row>
                <row><cell>1.5 ms</cell><cell>0°, the middle</cell></row>
                <row><cell>2 ms</cell><cell>+90°, the other end of the travel</cell></row>
            </tabular>
            <p>
                The endpoints are the convention, and a given servo does not
                necessarily reach them; the SG90 datasheet says approximately 180° of
                rotation.  Pulse widths outside 1 ms to 2 ms can drive the servo against
                its own stops, which is why the program limits every value it computes to
                <c>SERVO_MIN</c> through <c>SERVO_MAX</c> before writing <c>CCR1</c>.  The
                initialization is the one exception: <c>tim14_pa7_pwm_init()</c> parks
                <c>CCR1</c> at 0, so until the first potentiometer reading arrives there
                is no pulse at all and the servo is not commanded anywhere.
                The SG92R's dead band width is 1 µs: a change in pulse width smaller
                than that has no effect.  Its other specs, from the
                <url href="external/datasheets/C17481_SG92R_datasheet.pdf">datasheet</url>:
                stall torque 2.5 kgf-cm at 4.8 V, operating speed 0.1 s per 60°, operating
                voltage 4.8 V, POM and carbon-fiber gears.  It draws about 250 mA while
                moving, and much more when it is stalled.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-servo-ref-timer">
            <title>The Timer Arithmetic</title>
            <p>
                For a pulse period <m>T_p</m>, the counter divides each period into
                ARR + 1 counts, so the smallest pulse width, and the smallest change in
                pulse width, is <m>T_0 = T_p/(\text{ARR}+1)</m>.  The prescaler and the
                auto-reload value have to satisfy
                <m>\text{prescaleFactor} \times (\text{ARR}+1) = 12\text{ MHz} \times T_p</m>, with
                ARR at most 65,535.  The number of steps between 1 ms and 2 ms is
                <m>1\text{ ms}/T_0</m>, the servo's resolution counted in steps rather than
                in microseconds (<xref ref="subsec-day11x-resolution"/> measures it the
                other way), and the angle per step is 180° divided by that number, using the ±90° convention.  A step finer than the servo's dead band, or than its gear train's backlash,
                is more finely grained than is worth doing.  What is written into the registers is each
                number minus one, because the prescaler and the counter both start
                counting at 0: <c>TIM14->PSC = prescaleFactor-1</c> and
                <c>TIM14->ARR = timerMax-1</c>.  In PWM mode 1 the pin is HIGH while
                the counter is below <c>CCR1</c>, so <c>CCR1</c> is the number of
                counts the pin is HIGH.
            </p>
            <p>
                A program that needs a periodic tick while TIM14 is in use producing a PWM signal uses a second timer.  TIM16 (Reference Manual §18,
                <em>General-purpose timers (TIM16/TIM17)</em>) has the same prescaler,
                counter and auto-reload core, its interrupt is position 21 in the
                vector table, and its handler is <c>TIM16_IRQHandler()</c>.  The initialization is the one from Day 8 on a different timer, with the
                period as a parameter.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-servo-ref-driver">
            <title>The Driver: tim.c</title>
            <p>
                The whole of <c>tim.c</c>, with its comments shortened.  <c>tim14_pa7_pwm_init()</c> is the Day 11x
                driver with the two timer numbers as parameters, and its register
                writes are explained line by line in <xref ref="subsec-day11x-driver"/>.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "tim.h"
#include "ES28.h"

// Clock runs at 12MHz
#define TIM16_PSC_FACTOR	12000		// 12 MHz / 12000 = 1 kHz

void tim16_ms_interrupt_init(int milliseconds) {
	RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM16EN;	// clock access to timer 16
	TIM16->PSC = TIM16_PSC_FACTOR - 1;		// starts counting at 0
	TIM16->ARR = milliseconds - 1;
	TIM16->CNT = 0;
	TIM16->DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;			// update interrupt
	NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM16_IRQn);
	TIM16->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}

void tim14_pwm_set(uint16_t value) {
	// PWM mode 1: pin is HIGH while CNT < CCR1, i.e. for exactly 'value' counts
	TIM14->CCR1 = value;
}

void tim14_pa7_pwm_init(uint16_t prescaleFactor, uint16_t timerMax) {
	// Enable clock access to GPIOA
	RCC->IOPENR |=  RCC_IOPENR_GPIOAEN;

	// Set PA7 in alternate function mode (10)
	GPIOA->MODER &= ~GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Msk;
	GPIOA->MODER |= GPIO_ALTERNATE << GPIO_MODER_MODE7_Pos;

	// Set PA7 alternate function type (AF4, 0100)
	GPIOA->AFR[0] &= ~GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Msk;
	GPIOA->AFR[0] |= GPIO_AF4 << GPIO_AFRL_AFSEL7_Pos;

	// Enable clock access to Timer 14
	RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;

	// Set prescaler
	TIM14->PSC = prescaleFactor-1;

	// Set autoreload value (counter runs 0..timerMax-1: timerMax counts per period)
	TIM14->ARR = timerMax-1;

	// Set the compare register value - start with a speed of 0
	TIM14->CCR1 = 0;

	// Set pwm mode 1 (0110): pin is high up to capture value, then goes low
	TIM14->CCMR1 &= ~TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Msk;
	TIM14->CCMR1 |= TIM_OC1_PWM1 << TIM_CCMR1_OC1M_Pos;

	// Make sure timer is in output mode; this is default value
	TIM14->CCMR1 &= ~TIM_CCMR1_CC1S_Msk;
	TIM14->CCMR1 |= TIM_CC1_OUTPUT << TIM_CCMR1_CC1S_Pos;

	// Enable TIM14_CH1 in output mode
	TIM14->CCER |= TIM_CCER_CC1E;

	// Generate an update event - this clears counter, prescaler counter, updates registers
	TIM14->EGR |= TIM_EGR_UG;

	// Enable timer
	TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}
]]></code></program>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-servo-ref-map">
            <title>Scaling One Integer Range onto Another</title>
            <p>
                Two C facts matter whenever one integer range is scaled onto another.
                First, <c>/</c> on two integers discards the remainder, so a scale factor
                smaller than 1 written as a division comes out as 0; multiply before you
                divide.  Second, the product is computed in <c>int</c>, which is 32 bits
                on the STM32C031C6, so a 12-bit reading times a range of a few hundred
                fits easily (4095 × 200 = 819,000 is more than a 16-bit variable holds,
                and fits in an <c>int</c> with room to spare).  And if the divisor is the
                number of possible readings, 4096, rather than the largest reading, 4095,
                the top of the output range is one step short, which is usually fine and
                is worth knowing.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

</chapter>
